Best winter boots for Extreme Cold: SOREL Caribou

Insulation: 9 mm washable felt inner boot with snow cuffShaft height: 8.5 - 9” Temperature rating: -40°FWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Extreme cold, around town, working, outdoor choresWhat we liked: very warm, decade-long durability, sole has incredible tractionWhat we didn’t like: price, fit is not as snug due to the way the insulation is on the inside, can be too warm

The SOREL Caribou boots have long been considered the gold standard of extreme cold winter boots and have been kicking winter’s butt since the 1960s. And boy, can they pack a wallop. While known for their clunky-chore-boot charm, the Caribous have been keeping our toes ultra-toasty and feet dry in a 60-year spree with no end in sight.

For more info, see our in-depth review of the SOREL Caribou boots.

The SOREL Caribou boots are incredibly warm and provide enough insulation for sinking in snow over and over again.

We love that the SOREL Caribou are durable enough to last decades, as this men’s pair still in use since the 1990s.

The quality rubber sole on the SOREL Caribou boots and deep lugs mean that you have traction even in icier conditions.

The felt liners in the SOREL Caribou are removable so you can clean or replace them if needed, as we’ve done with this men’s pair.

The pull tab in the back of the SOREL Caribou is sturdy and makes it easier to take these boots on and off.

My father has owned his pair of SOREL Caribous since 1992. I’ve never seen a winter without him wearing those boots—from New York to Colorado to Maine! In fact, many reviewers brought up family members having owned these boots for 15+ years, with the boots still going strong.

The women’s version of the Caribou is just as warm and durable. These boots are rated by SOREL down to -40°F. The author can tell you they’re quite comfy down to at least -20°F. Three different Treeline Review testers can verify, having used the SOREL Caribou from Maine to Colorado to Washington.

The tread on the Caribous offered some of the best grip on packed snow, while the felt cuff and mid-calf shaft kept the snow, slush, and puddles far away from reviewers’ feet. For these reasons, the Caribou boots won our award for best women’s winter boots for extreme cold.

Best women's winter boots for walking: L.L.Bean Women’s 8”Bean Boots

Insulation: Available in: PrimaLoft, shearling liningShaft height: 8”Temperature rating: -30FWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Around town, styleWhat we liked: style, warmth, durability, made in US, variety of heights, colors, insulation, and sizes availableWhat we didn’t like: can be hard to get due to high demand, price

It’s a given; we couldn’t ignore the classic L.L.Bean Duck Boot. With a history dating back to 1912, these made in the USA boots can be backordered by the 100,000.

The Bean Boot has a classic look that works in urban settings or on a trail. It's comfortable enough for walking commutes and wearing all-day in the office.

It’s a look that has been mimicked by boot makers—from J.Crew on the fancy end to XTRATUF on the outdoorsy side. It’s not only comfortable and functional, but it has an aesthetic that works for a lot of settings—and we think that is a factor that makes it a winning boot.

There are a lot of contenders for the best winter boots for walking. But ultimately, we think the L.L. Bean Boot is a better value than most and is still made in the USA.

For more information, see our in-depth review of the L.L.Bean Boots.

That being said, while we’ve tromped through towns, down docks, and across snowy hills in our Bean duck boots, we wouldn’t recommend going on an extended hike with them right out of the box. The leather can be stiff and needs time to break in and mold to your feet.

When it comes to sizing, we recommend that half sizes order 0.5 sizes down (if you wear light or midweight socks). L.L.Bean has a great and easy exchange policy, however, so if your boots don’t fit right the first time, don’t be afraid to swap them out.

Best non slip winter boots: Oboz Bridger 9” Insulated B-DRY Winter Boots

Insulation: 400 g Freeze Guard insulationShaft height: 9” Temperature rating: Not statedWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Hiking, snowshoeing, workingWhat we liked: excellent traction on ice, excellent ankle supportWhat we didn’t like: can be hard to find this high-demand boot, sizing runs small, can feel too stiff for city use

If neither ice nor snow will keep you off the trails, these boots are for you. If ice just isn’t your thing walking into work, these are also your perfect boots. The Oboz Bridger 9” Insulated Winter Boots are undoubtedly a chilly winter hiker’s dream—and one we found useful for shoveling snow, walking the dog, and winter activities where good traction is essential.

Oboz is primarily a hiking boot and hiking shoe company, and you’ll see them in our Best Hiking Boots and Best Hiking Shoes guide. To make their winter hiking boot, they took the design and technology and added extra height and insulation.

We chose the Oboz Bridger 9” as the best non slip winter boots due to the excellent traction and grip that the Oboz Bridgers have on slippery, icy, and snowy surfaces. The Oboz Bridger is also our winner in our Best Winter Hiking Boots guide.

For more information, see our long-term review of the Oboz Bridger 9" Winter Boots.

The Oboz Bridger winter hiking boots on an early winter day in Maine.

We were impressed by the traction on the Oboz Bridger on slippery, wet rocks and other obstacles we encountered while hiking.

Close-up of the winterized rubber outsole on the Oboz Bridger 9-inch Insulated boots.

The Oboz Bridger insulated boots also work well in deep mud (and clean off easily, too).

Close-up on the protective toe cap on the Oboz Bridger Insulated boots.

The author tested the Oboz Bridger in deeper snow in Maine and New Hampshire and appreciated the high lacing to really get the fit dialed in, especially with thicker socks.

In testing, we found the Oboz Bridger is also incredibly warm — with 400 grams of Freeze Guard insulation. With 3 mm thickness, that Freeze Guard insulation is more than enough insulation to keep even the coldest toes cozy. It’s also ringed by wool on the top. You may even find these boots too warm!

One potential ding against the Oboz Bridger is that it’s a stiffer boot than most hiking boots. (With the insulation, waterproof rubber, and waterproof nubuck leather, it’s not a crazy claim.) However, if ankle support is an important aspect of winter boots for you, the stiffness of the Oboz Bridger is actually a plus. They also provide great ankle support by lacing below and above the ankle — another perk of their height.

While this wasn't our testers' experience, customer reviewers also warned that the Oboz Bridgers tend to run small, so sizing up a half size isn’t a bad idea, especially if you are wearing thick winter socks.

Best Winter Hiking Boots: The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid Waterproof Hiking Shoe

Insulation: 200 g Heatseeker Synthetic InsulationShaft height: Mid (4-6”, depending on size)Temperature rating: Not stated, 10-20F in testingWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Hiking, winter backpacking, snowshoeingWhat we liked: Comfortable, lightweight, good groundfeel, thoughtful hiking-specific featuresWhat we didn’t like: When fully saturated, the midfoot and toe box protection was somewhat compromised, can be hard to find the women's version

If you want a lightweight hiking boot with insulation and waterproofing, the best winter hiking boots are The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid. These boots are hands-down the favorite of hikers who prefer wearing mesh lightweight trail running shoes in the summer but find they need insulation and waterproofing in the winter.

In our testing, they keep you warm down to 10-20F, just as long as you are moving. They are designed for movement in snow and ice. These boots have some unique features, like a rocker midsole to smooth out the hiking gait, a TPU plate to enhance propulsion and protect the forefoot from rocks, a knob on the heel to keep snowshoe straps from sliding down, and pull tabs on both the tongue and heel to make it easy to pull the boots on.

The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Hiking shoe is the overall winner in our Best Winter Hiking Boots guide.

The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid boots kept our feet warm while snowshoeing in winter conditions, with 200g Heatseeker insulation and a waterproof construction.

A hiker wears The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid on packed snow, where the lightweight design feels more like a trail shoe than a traditional winter boot.

The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid was tested on overnight snowshoeing trips, where its insulation and waterproofing make it well suited to winter backcountry travel.

The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid boots dry beside a wood stove after a day in the snow, part of our winter testing in conditions ranging from cold and snowy to warm and rainy.

The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid combines a lightweight hiking-shoe feel with the higher collar, insulation, and protection needed for winter trails.

A hiker snowshoes uphill in The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid, a lightweight winter boot designed to stay comfortable over long miles.

The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid has a heel knob designed to keep a snowshoe strap from slipping down while hiking.

A close-up of The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid in a snowshoe shows how the heel design helps hold the rear strap securely in place.

The boot’s soft collar includes a fleece fabric that creates a comfortable closure around the lower leg and helps make long miles in these boots very comfortable. The North Face uses only Leather Working Group tanneries, sourcing their leather from approved tanners that reduce water and energy use. These boots use Heatseeker Eco insulation which is composed of 70-85% post-consumer recycled materials and sources 10% of the outsole’s rubber from small farmers committed to agroforestry principles.

We've tested The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated on numerous overnight snowshoeing trips in the Pacific Northwest. We also tested it on a 3-night, 4-day winter trip into Baxter State Park in Maine. Over the course of the trip, the tester summited Katahdin, Hamlin, and North Brother, which are three peaks on the New England 67 4,000-footers list, and notoriously difficult in winter. These boots performed perfectly in a wide variety of temperatures, from 15-degree cold to a 45-degree rainy day.

Many hikers may prefer the stability and protection of the Oboz Bridger 9" for hiking. However, if that is too much boot for you, the best winter boot for hiking is The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated.

Best Affordable Women’s Winter Boot: Columbia Ice Maiden II

Insulation: 200 g insulation, microfleece liner, faux fur collarShaft height: 9” Temperature rating: -25FWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Around town, styleWhat we liked: style, price, enough warmth without being overkillWhat we didn’t like: not the boots for the coldest climates, could have better traction

Want a warm winter boot that will keep out the snow without breaking the bank? Look no further than the Columbia Ice Maiden II. The Ice Maiden IIs can be found from $45 to 110, depending on which color you prefer. It’s one of the least expensive options anywhere—yet still managed to get good marks on all of our criteria for what to look for in a snow boot (see our standards below). There’s a reason why it’s a bestseller at Amazon for women’s mid-calf winter boots.

The price tag doesn’t compromise a lot. The Ice Maiden IIs have 200 grams of insulation, a microfleece liner, and a faux fur collar, as well as a waterproof suede and nylon upper. The sole is thick enough to keep out the cold without becoming overkill, too. While it’s not the warmest boot, nor the most rock-solid waterproof boot on the market, these are the boots to have on hand if you’re traveling to a ski resort for one week each year or just want something simple and warm to keep you cozy while you’re running errands or walking the dog.

The Ice Maiden IIs have been described as “true to size,” so unless you plan to wear oversized socks, stick to your street shoe size. There’s ample room to wiggle toes, which helps contribute to the warmth factor, too! There weren’t significant complaints about toe or heel rubbing.

The Ice Maiden IIs are stiffer boots. It's not what we would take out for any major hikes but they are flexible and supportive enough for everyday outings.

At 9” high, the Ice Maiden IIs are tall enough to keep out the majority of everyday snow piles and puddles. What moisture the height won’t catch, the faux fur ruff will. Without being too fluffy, the fur lining catches water and snow that might otherwise try to jump into your boot.

While the tread on the Ice Maiden IIs is up to the task of walking around town, these boots are not meant for extended forays in icy terrain. Without deep tread, they’re not meant for big snowpacks and large patches of ice.

All in all, these boots can get the job done without going over budget. We think that’s a win-win.

Best Insulated Winter Boots: BOGS Neo-Classic Tall

